            <ArticleTitle>Quantitative Frequency-Domain Passive Cavitation Imaging.</ArticleTitle>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1109/TUFFC.2016.2620492</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Passive cavitation detection has been an instrumental technique for measuring cavitation dynamics, elucidating concomitant bioeffects, and guiding ultrasound therapies. Recently, techniques have been developed to create images of cavitation activity to provide investigators with a more complete set of information. These techniques use arrays to record and subsequently beamform received cavitation emissions, rather than processing emissions received on a single-element transducer. In this paper, the methods for performing frequency-domain delay, sum, and integrate passive imaging are outlined. The method can be applied to any passively acquired acoustic scattering or emissions, including cavitation emissions. In order to compare data across different systems, techniques for normalizing Fourier transformed data and converting the data to the acoustic power received by the array are described. A discussion of hardware requirements and alternative imaging approaches are additionally outlined. Examples are provided in MATLAB.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
